const units = [
['Kinematics','Position, velocity & acceleration','x(t) → v(t) → a(t)',[
['A particle has position x(t) = 3t² + 2t (SI units). What is its velocity at t = 2 s?',['8 m/s','12 m/s','14 m/s','16 m/s'],2,'Differentiate position: v(t) = 6t + 2. At 2 s, v = 14 m/s.'],
['A particle starts from rest with acceleration a(t) = 6t m/s². Its displacement in the first 2 s is:',['4 m','8 m','12 m','24 m'],1,'Integrate a to obtain v = 3t², then integrate again: Δx = t³. At 2 s, Δx = 8 m.'],
['A projectile is launched at speed v₀ at angle θ (45° < θ < 90°) from level ground. Neglect air resistance. At what time is its velocity perpendicular to its initial velocity?',['v₀/(g sin θ)','v₀ sin θ/g','v₀ cos θ/g','2v₀ sin θ/g'],0,'Set v(t) · v(0) = 0: v₀² − gv₀t sin θ = 0, so t = v₀/(g sin θ). This occurs during flight only if sin²θ ≥ 1/2.']]],
['Force and Translational Dynamics','Free-body diagrams, forces & gravitation','ΣF = ma',[
['A 2 kg block on a frictionless horizontal surface experiences a net horizontal force of 6 N. Its acceleration is:',['2 m/s²','3 m/s²','6 m/s²','12 m/s²'],1,'Newton’s second law gives a = F/m = 6/2 = 3 m/s².'],
['A mass slides down an incline of angle θ with kinetic friction coefficient μ. Its acceleration down the incline is:',['g sin θ','g(cos θ − μ sin θ)','g(sin θ − μ cos θ)','g(sin θ + μ cos θ)'],2,'Along the slope, mg sin θ − μmg cos θ = ma. The normal force is mg cos θ.'],
['A mass m falls from rest under gravity and a linear drag force −bv. Taking down as positive, its speed at time t is:',['(mg/b)(1 − e^(−bt/m))','gt e^(−bt/m)','(mg/b)e^(−bt/m)','(mg/b)(1 − e^(−mt/b))'],0,'Solve m dv/dt = mg − bv with v(0)=0. The terminal speed is mg/b and the time constant is m/b.']]],
['Work, Energy, and Power','Energy transfers & conservation','W = ∫ F · dr',[
['A 2 kg object speeds up from 1 m/s to 3 m/s. The net work done is:',['4 J','6 J','8 J','10 J'],2,'W = ΔK = ½(2)(3² − 1²) = 8 J.'],
['A force F(x) = kx² acts along +x. The work from x = 0 to x = L is:',['kL²','kL³/3','kL³/2','2kL'],1,'W = ∫₀ᴸ kx² dx = kL³/3. The area under the force–position graph is the work.'],
['A particle has potential U(x) = ax⁴ − bx², where a,b > 0. Which positions are stable equilibria?',['x = 0 only','x = ±√(b/a)','x = ±√(b/(2a))','All positions where U = 0'],2,'Equilibrium requires dU/dx = 4ax³ − 2bx = 0. At ±√(b/(2a)), U″ = 12ax² − 2b = 4b > 0, so these are minima.']]],
['Linear Momentum','Impulse, collisions & systems','J = ∫ F dt = Δp',[
['A 3 kg cart moving at 4 m/s sticks to a stationary 1 kg cart. Their common speed is:',['1 m/s','2 m/s','3 m/s','4 m/s'],2,'Conserve momentum: 3(4) = (3+1)v, giving v = 3 m/s. Kinetic energy is not conserved.'],
['A force F(t) = F₀ sin(πt/T) acts from t = 0 to T. Its impulse is:',['0','F₀T','F₀T/π','2F₀T/π'],3,'J = ∫₀ᵀ F₀ sin(πt/T) dt = 2F₀T/π.'],
['A mass m moving at speed v elastically strikes a stationary mass 3m in one dimension. The initial mass’s final velocity is:',['−v/2','v/2','−v','0'],0,'For a 1D elastic collision, v₁f = (m₁−m₂)v₁i/(m₁+m₂) = −v/2. Momentum and kinetic energy are both conserved.']]],
['Torque and Rotational Dynamics','Angular motion, torque & inertia','Στ = Iα',[
['A tangential 5 N force acts at a radius of 0.4 m. The torque magnitude is:',['0.8 N·m','2 N·m','5.4 N·m','12.5 N·m'],1,'τ = rF sin 90° = 0.4(5) = 2 N·m.'],
['A uniform rod of mass M and length L pivots about one end. Released from rest horizontally, its initial angular acceleration is:',['g/L','3g/(2L)','2g/L','g/(2L)'],1,'Torque about the end is MgL/2, and I = ML²/3. Thus α = τ/I = 3g/(2L).'],
['A thin rod extends from x = 0 to L with linear density λ(x) = cx. Its moment of inertia about an axis through x = 0 perpendicular to the rod is:',['ML²/3','ML²','ML²/2','ML²/4'],2,'M = ∫₀ᴸ cx dx = cL²/2. I = ∫₀ᴸ x²(cx) dx = cL⁴/4 = ML²/2.']]],
['Energy and Momentum of Rotating Systems','Rolling, angular momentum & orbits','L = Iω',[
['A skater halves her moment of inertia with negligible external torque. Her angular speed:',['Halves','Stays constant','Doubles','Quadruples'],2,'Iᵢωᵢ = I_fω_f. Halving I doubles ω.'],
['A solid sphere rolls without slipping down a vertical height h from rest. Its center-of-mass speed is:',['√(2gh)','√(gh)','√(10gh/7)','√(4gh/3)'],2,'mgh = ½mv² + ½(2mr²/5)(v/r)² = 7mv²/10. Thus v = √(10gh/7).'],
['A small clay lump of mass m and speed v strikes tangentially and sticks to the rim of a stationary disk of mass M and radius R on a frictionless fixed axle. Final angular speed is:',['mv/((M+m)R)','mv/((M/2+m)R)','2v/R','mv/(MR)'],1,'Angular momentum about the axle is conserved: mvR = (MR²/2 + mR²)ω. The collision is inelastic.']]],
['Oscillations','Springs, pendulums & periodic motion','ẍ + ω²x = 0',[
['The mass on an ideal spring is quadrupled. The oscillation period:',['Halves','Doubles','Quadruples','Stays constant'],1,'T = 2π√(m/k), so multiplying m by 4 doubles T.'],
['An oscillator has amplitude A and angular frequency ω. At x = A/2, its speed is:',['ωA/2','ωA√3/2','ωA','ωA/√2'],1,'Energy conservation gives v² = ω²(A²−x²). At x = A/2, v = √3 ωA/2.'],
['A uniform rod of length L swings with small amplitude about one end. Its period is:',['2π√(L/g)','2π√(3L/(2g))','2π√(2L/(3g))','2π√(L/(3g))'],2,'For a physical pendulum, T = 2π√(I/(Mgd)). Use I = ML²/3 and d = L/2 to get T = 2π√(2L/(3g)).']]],
['Electric Charges, Fields, and Gauss’s Law','Charge distributions, symmetry & flux','∮ E · dA = Qenc/ε₀',[
['The distance from an isolated point charge doubles. The electric field magnitude becomes:',['2E','E/2','E/4','4E'],2,'E = k|Q|/r². Doubling r reduces E by a factor of four.'],
['An infinite line carries uniform positive charge density λ. The field at distance r is:',['λ/(4πε₀r²)','λ/(2πε₀r)','λr/(2ε₀)','λ/ε₀'],1,'Use a coaxial Gaussian cylinder: E(2πrℓ) = λℓ/ε₀, giving E = λ/(2πε₀r), outward.'],
['A solid sphere of radius R has volume charge density ρ(r) = ar. For r < R, the field magnitude is:',['ar/(3ε₀)','ar²/(4ε₀)','aR²/(4ε₀)','ar³/(4ε₀)'],1,'Qenc = ∫₀ʳ (ar′)4πr′² dr′ = πar⁴. Gauss’s law gives E = Qenc/(4πε₀r²) = ar²/(4ε₀).']]],
['Electric Potential','Potential, fields & electric energy','E = −∇V',[
['A charge +2 C moves through a potential increase of 3 V. Its potential energy changes by:',['−6 J','1.5 J','5 J','6 J'],3,'ΔU = qΔV = (+2)(+3) = +6 J.'],
['In a region V(x) = ax², what is the x-component of electric field?',['ax','−2ax','2ax','−ax²'],1,'Eₓ = −dV/dx = −2ax. The field points toward decreasing potential.'],
['A uniformly charged ring of radius R has charge Q. On its axis at position z, the axial field is:',['kQ/(R²+z²)','kQz/(R²+z²)^(3/2)','kQ/(R+z)²','kQR/(R²+z²)^(3/2)'],1,'V(z) = kQ/√(R²+z²). Then E_z = −dV/dz = kQz/(R²+z²)^(3/2).']]],
['Conductors and Capacitors','Electrostatic equilibrium & capacitance','C = Q/ΔV',[
['Inside the material of a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium, the electric field is:',['Zero','Uniform and nonzero','Infinite','Dependent only on its mass'],0,'Free charges redistribute until the field inside the conducting material vanishes.'],
['A parallel-plate capacitor stays connected to an ideal battery. A dielectric of constant κ fully fills the gap. Stored energy becomes:',['U/κ','κU','κ²U','U'],1,'Voltage stays fixed and C becomes κC. U = ½CV² therefore becomes κU; the battery supplies charge.'],
['An isolated charged parallel-plate capacitor has initial energy U. Its plate separation is doubled, neglecting fringing. New energy is:',['U/2','U','2U','4U'],2,'Charge is fixed. Since C = ε₀A/d, doubling d halves C. U = Q²/(2C) doubles; external work supplies the increase.']]],
['Electric Circuits','Current, resistance & RC circuits','V = IR',[
['A 12 V ideal battery is connected across a 4 Ω resistor. Current is:',['0.33 A','3 A','8 A','48 A'],1,'Ohm’s law gives I = V/R = 12/4 = 3 A.'],
['An initially uncharged capacitor C charges through R from an ideal source ℰ. At t = RC, its charge is:',['Cℰ','Cℰ/e','Cℰ(1−1/e)','0'],2,'q(t) = Cℰ(1−e^(−t/RC)). After one time constant it has about 63% of its final charge.'],
['A wire of length L has cross-sectional area A(x) = A₀(1+x/L) and uniform resistivity ρ. Its resistance is:',['ρL/A₀','2ρL/A₀','ρL ln 2/A₀','ρL/(2A₀)'],2,'Add differential series resistances: R = ∫₀ᴸ ρ dx/[A₀(1+x/L)] = (ρL/A₀)ln 2.']]],
['Magnetic Fields and Electromagnetism','Magnetic forces & sources of B','F = qv × B',[
['A charged particle travels parallel to a uniform magnetic field. The magnetic force is:',['qvB','Zero','qB/v','mv²/r'],1,'F = |q|vB sin θ, and θ = 0 for parallel motion.'],
['A positive charge q moves perpendicular to uniform B with speed v. Its circular orbit radius is:',['qB/(mv)','mv/(qB)','mB/(qv)','qv/(mB)'],1,'Magnetic force provides centripetal force: qvB = mv²/r, so r = mv/(qB).'],
['A long solid wire of radius R carries current I uniformly across its cross section. For r < R, B(r) is:',['μ₀I/(2πr)','μ₀Ir/(2πR²)','μ₀I/(2πR)','μ₀Ir²/(2πR³)'],1,'Ampère’s law gives B(2πr)=μ₀Ienc with Ienc=Ir²/R², hence B=μ₀Ir/(2πR²).']]],
['Electromagnetic Induction','Changing flux, inductance & LR circuits','ℰ = −dΦB/dt',[
['Magnetic flux through a single stationary loop increases uniformly by 0.6 Wb in 0.2 s. The induced emf magnitude is:',['0.12 V','0.3 V','3 V','6 V'],2,'|ℰ| = |ΔΦ/Δt| = 0.6/0.2 = 3 V.'],
['A rod of length ℓ slides at speed v perpendicular to a uniform B, with the rod perpendicular to both v and B. Its motional emf magnitude is:',['Bℓ/v','Bv/ℓ','Bℓv','Bℓv²'],2,'Charges separate until qE = qvB. The potential difference Eℓ is Bℓv.'],
['An ideal battery ℰ is switched onto a series RL circuit at t=0. How much energy is stored in the inductor at t=L/R?',['Lℰ²/(2R²)','(Lℰ²/(2R²))(1−e⁻¹)²','(Lℰ²/(2R²))e⁻²','Lℰ²/R²'],1,'I(t)=(ℰ/R)(1−e^(−Rt/L)). Substitute t=L/R into U=½LI² to obtain the squared (1−e⁻¹) factor.']]]
];
const frqs = [
[['A cart moves with x(t)=2t³−3t²+4 (m), t≥0.','Find v(t) and a(t).','Find the times when the cart is at rest and its displacement from t=0 to 2 s.','v=6t²−6t; a=12t−6.','v=0 at t=0 and 1 s. Δx=x(2)−x(0)=4 m.'],['A particle starts at x=0 with v₀=2 m/s and a(t)=4t (SI units).','Derive v(t) and x(t).','Find its average velocity from 0 to 3 s.','v=2+2t²; x=2t+(2/3)t³.','x(3)=24 m, so average velocity=24/3=8 m/s.'],['A projectile leaves level ground at speed v₀ and angle θ; ignore drag.','Derive the trajectory y(x) by eliminating time.','Use the trajectory to find the range and the angle that maximizes it.','x=v₀cosθ t, y=v₀sinθ t−gt²/2, so y=x tanθ−gx²/(2v₀²cos²θ).','Set y=0: R=v₀²sin(2θ)/g. Its maximum occurs at θ=45°.']],
[['A 4 kg block rests on a frictionless horizontal surface. A horizontal 12 N force is applied.','Describe the free-body diagram and calculate the acceleration.','Find its speed after 3 s, starting from rest.','Weight 4g downward, normal 4g upward, applied force 12 N horizontally; a=3 m/s².','v=at=9 m/s.'],['A mass m slides down a slope of angle θ with coefficient of kinetic friction μ.','Derive its acceleration using a free-body diagram.','Determine the condition for its speed to increase while moving downhill.','N=mg cosθ; ma=mg sinθ−μmg cosθ, so a=g(sinθ−μcosθ).','Speed increases downhill if a>0, requiring tanθ>μ.'],['A mass m is released from rest in a medium with drag magnitude bv. Take downward as positive.','Solve for velocity as a function of time.','Derive distance fallen and check the short-time limit.','m dv/dt=mg−bv; v=(mg/b)(1−e^(−bt/m)).','y=(mg/b)[t−(m/b)(1−e^(−bt/m))]. Expanding the exponential gives y≈gt²/2.']],
[['A 2 kg block starts from rest and falls 5 m without air resistance.','Find the change in gravitational potential energy.','Find its speed just before impact using energy.','ΔU=−mgh=−10g J.','½mv²=mgh, so v=√(10g), about 9.9 m/s.'],['A particle of mass m starts from rest at x=0 under F(x)=ax², a>0.','Calculate work to reach x=L.','Derive its speed at L.','W=∫₀ᴸ ax² dx=aL³/3.','½mv²=aL³/3, so v=√(2aL³/(3m)).'],['A particle of mass m moves in U(x)=ax⁴−bx², a,b>0.','Find and classify all equilibrium positions.','Find the angular frequency of small oscillations about a stable point.','U′=0 at 0 and ±√(b/(2a)); U″(0)=−2b so 0 is unstable, while U″=4b at the other two so they are stable.','Near a minimum U≈Umin+½(4b)ξ². Hence ω=√(4b/m).']],
[['A 2 kg cart traveling at 6 m/s sticks to a stationary 4 kg cart.','Find the common final velocity.','Calculate the kinetic energy lost.','Momentum: 12=6v, so v=2 m/s in the original direction.','Kᵢ=36 J; Kf=12 J; loss=24 J.'],['A force F(t)=F₀t/T acts on a mass m initially at rest for 0≤t≤T.','Find the impulse.','Find the final speed and kinetic energy.','J=∫₀ᵀ F₀t/T dt=F₀T/2.','v=F₀T/(2m); K=F₀²T²/(8m).'],['A projectile of mass m and speed v embeds in a hanging block of mass M.','Derive their speed immediately after impact.','Derive the maximum rise and explain the conservation laws used.','During the brief impact horizontal momentum is conserved: V=mv/(m+M).','After impact mechanical energy is conserved: h=V²/(2g)=m²v²/[2g(m+M)²]. Energy is not conserved during embedding.']],
[['A wheel has moment of inertia 2 kg·m² and constant net torque 6 N·m.','Find angular acceleration.','Find angular speed after 4 s from rest.','α=τ/I=3 rad/s².','ω=αt=12 rad/s.'],['A uniform rod of mass M and length L pivots about one end and is released horizontally.','Derive initial angular acceleration.','Find the initial tangential acceleration of its free end.','τ=MgL/2 and I=ML²/3 give α=3g/(2L).','a_t=αL=3g/2 downward; the pivot exerts a constraint force.'],['A rod from x=0 to L has λ(x)=cx. It rotates about its left end.','Express c in terms of total mass M and find its center of mass.','Find the moment of inertia about the left end.','M=cL²/2 so c=2M/L²; xcm=(1/M)∫x(cx)dx=2L/3.','I=∫x²(cx)dx=cL⁴/4=ML²/2.']],
[['A disk with I=3 kg·m² rotates at 4 rad/s.','Calculate angular momentum and kinetic energy.','If its moment of inertia doubles with zero external torque, find final speed.','L=Iω=12 kg·m²/s; K=½Iω²=24 J.','Angular momentum stays 12, so ωf=12/6=2 rad/s.'],['A uniform solid cylinder rolls from rest down a vertical height h without slipping.','Derive its final center-of-mass speed.','Find the fraction of kinetic energy in rotation.','mgh=½mv²+½(mR²/2)(v/R)²=3mv²/4, giving v=√(4gh/3).','Krot=mv²/4 and Ktotal=3mv²/4, so the fraction is 1/3.'],['A lump of clay m moving tangentially at speed v sticks to a disk rim R. The disk mass is M and its axle is frictionless and fixed.','Derive the final angular speed.','Find the ratio of final kinetic energy to initial kinetic energy.','mvR=(MR²/2+mR²)ω, giving ω=mv/[(M/2+m)R].','Kf/Kᵢ=m/(M/2+m). Angular momentum about the axle is conserved, but kinetic energy decreases.']],
[['A 0.5 kg mass oscillates on an ideal spring with k=8 N/m and amplitude 0.1 m.','Find the angular frequency and period.','Find the maximum speed.','ω=√(k/m)=4 rad/s; T=2π/ω=π/2 s.','vmax=ωA=0.4 m/s.'],['An oscillator begins at x=A with v=0 at t=0. Its mass is m and spring constant k.','Write x(t) and v(t).','Find the first time that kinetic and potential energies are equal.','x=A cos(ωt), v=−Aω sin(ωt), with ω=√(k/m).','Equal energies imply sin²(ωt)=cos²(ωt); first time t=π/(4ω)=T/8.'],['A uniform rod of length L swings through small angles about one end.','Derive its small-angle equation of motion.','Find the period and compare it with a point pendulum of length L.','Iθ̈=−Mg(L/2)sinθ≈−MgLθ/2. With I=ML²/3, θ̈+(3g/(2L))θ=0.','T=2π√(2L/(3g)); it is √(2/3) times the point-pendulum period.']],
[['A point charge Q>0 is isolated in vacuum.','Write the field at distance r and its direction.','Find the flux through a sphere centered on Q.','E=Q/(4πε₀r²), radially outward.','Flux=E4πr²=Q/ε₀, independent of radius.'],['An infinitely long line has positive charge per unit length λ.','Choose a Gaussian surface and derive E(r).','Explain why a finite line does not have the same field everywhere.','Use a coaxial cylinder length ℓ: end-cap flux is zero, curved-surface flux=E2πrℓ=λℓ/ε₀; E=λ/(2πε₀r).','A finite line lacks translational symmetry; near its ends the field has an axial component and the infinite-line formula fails.'],['A solid sphere of radius R has ρ(r)=ar, a>0.','Derive E(r) inside the sphere.','Derive E(r) outside and check continuity at R.','Qenc=4πa∫₀ʳ r′³dr′=πar⁴, so E=ar²/(4ε₀) outward.','Q=πaR⁴ so E=aR⁴/(4ε₀r²) outside. Both give aR²/(4ε₀) at R.']],
[['A +3 μC charge moves from V=10 V to V=40 V.','Find its potential-energy change.','Find the work done by the electric field.','ΔU=qΔV=3×10⁻⁶(30)=9×10⁻⁵ J.','Wfield=−ΔU=−9×10⁻⁵ J.'],['The potential along x is V(x)=ax², a>0.','Derive Eₓ(x).','Find the work done by the field moving a positive charge q from 0 to L.','Eₓ=−dV/dx=−2ax.','W=q[V(0)−V(L)]=−qaL². The field opposes motion along positive x.'],['A ring of radius R carries uniform charge Q>0. Let z be distance along its axis.','Derive V(z) and E_z(z), taking V=0 at infinity.','Find the positive z at which E_z is largest.','Every charge element is distance √(R²+z²) away: V=kQ/√(R²+z²); E_z=kQz/(R²+z²)^(3/2).','dE_z/dz=kQ(R²−2z²)/(R²+z²)^(5/2). Thus the maximum is at z=R/√2.']],
[['A capacitor C is charged to voltage V.','Write its charge and stored energy.','Two identical such capacitors are connected in parallel. Find equivalent capacitance.','Q=CV; U=½CV².','Ceq=C+C=2C because the plates share the same voltage.'],['A parallel-plate capacitor remains attached to an ideal battery of voltage V. Insert a dielectric κ completely.','Find the new capacitance and charge in terms of initial values.','Find the change in stored energy and explain the energy source.','Cf=κC₀ and Qf=κC₀V=κQ₀.','Uf=κU₀; ΔU=(κ−1)U₀. The battery supplies energy as extra charge flows. Some battery energy also becomes mechanical work during insertion.'],['An isolated capacitor has plate area A, gap d, and charge Q. Ignore fringing. Increase the gap to 2d slowly.','Find initial and final energies.','Calculate the required external work and relate it to force.','Ci=ε₀A/d, Cf=Ci/2. Ui=Q²d/(2ε₀A), Uf=Q²d/(ε₀A).','Wext=Uf−Ui=Q²d/(2ε₀A). The attractive force magnitude Q²/(2ε₀A) is constant; Wext=Fd.']],
[['An ideal 9 V battery drives a 3 Ω resistor.','Find the current.','Find the dissipated power.','I=V/R=3 A.','P=VI=27 W (also I²R).'],['An initially uncharged capacitor C is charged through R by a battery ℰ.','Derive q(t) from Kirchhoff’s loop rule.','Find the current at t=0 and at t=RC.','ℰ−R dq/dt−q/C=0 yields q=Cℰ(1−e^(−t/RC)).','I=(ℰ/R)e^(−t/RC), so I(0)=ℰ/R and I(RC)=ℰ/(eR).'],['A wire of length L has uniform resistivity ρ and A(x)=A₀(1+x/L).','Derive its total resistance.','For steady current I, find E(x) and integrate it to verify the voltage drop.','R=∫₀ᴸ ρ dx/A(x)=(ρL/A₀)ln2.','J=I/A(x) so E=ρI/[A₀(1+x/L)]. ∫₀ᴸ E dx=(ρIL/A₀)ln2=IR.']],
[['A charge q>0 moves at speed v perpendicular to uniform B.','Give the force magnitude and describe its direction.','Explain why the field does no work.','F=qvB, with direction v×B given by the right-hand rule.','The magnetic force is perpendicular to velocity, so F·v=0 and speed stays constant.'],['A positive charge q of mass m enters a uniform B perpendicular to the field at speed v.','Derive its orbit radius.','Find the orbit period and explain its dependence on speed.','qvB=mv²/r, so r=mv/(qB).','T=2πr/v=2πm/(qB), independent of speed in the nonrelativistic limit.'],['A long solid wire of radius R carries uniform current I.','Derive B(r) for r<R.','Derive B(r) for r>R and describe continuity at R.','Ienc=I r²/R². Ampère’s law gives B=μ₀Ir/(2πR²).','Outside, Ienc=I and B=μ₀I/(2πr). Both equal μ₀I/(2πR) at the surface.']],
[['A stationary single-turn loop has area A, perpendicular to B(t)=bt with b>0.','Find the magnitude of induced emf.','Describe the induced current’s field direction.','Φ=Abt so |ℰ|=Ab.','By Lenz’s law the induced field opposes the increasing applied flux.'],['A rod of length ℓ slides on conducting rails at speed v through perpendicular field B. Total circuit resistance is R.','Derive the current and magnetic drag force magnitude.','Show that mechanical power equals electrical heating.','ℰ=Bℓv; I=Bℓv/R. F=IℓB=B²ℓ²v/R, opposite motion.','Pext=Fv=B²ℓ²v²/R=I²R.'],['A battery ℰ is connected at t=0 to series inductance L and resistance R with I(0)=0.','Derive I(t).','Find the energy stored at t=L/R and the long-time energy.','L dI/dt+RI=ℰ; I=(ℰ/R)(1−e^(−Rt/L)).','U=½LI². At t=L/R, U=Lℰ²(1−e⁻¹)²/(2R²); as t→∞, U=Lℰ²/(2R²).']]
];
const bank=[];
units.forEach((u,i)=>u[3].forEach((q,d)=>{bank.push({id:`${i+1}-m-${d}`,unit:i+1,type:'MCQ',level:d,...{prompt:q[0],options:q[1],answer:q[2],explanation:q[3]}});const f=frqs[i][d];bank.push({id:`${i+1}-f-${d}`,unit:i+1,type:'FRQ',level:d,prompt:f[0],parts:[f[1],f[2]],solutions:[f[3],f[4]]});}));
